WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:03.084
Welcome to SysNDD.

00:03.217 --> 00:07.710
Let's explore gene networks,
and where phenotypes meet them.

00:08.233 --> 00:14.266
Which SysNDD genes work together,
and do their disorders look alike?

00:14.399 --> 00:19.993
We look at the network, one cluster,
its summary and the phenotypes.

00:20.500 --> 00:26.610
Under Analyses,
Functional clusters shows a network of SysNDD genes,

00:26.744 --> 00:30.754
linked by protein interactions from the STRING database.

00:30.888 --> 00:33.293
Each color is one cluster.

00:33.427 --> 00:36.807
The view starts with genes of Definitive entities;

00:36.941 --> 00:40.340
the Category menu adds the others.

00:40.867 --> 00:47.020
The clusters use every gene with an entity
for a neurodevelopmental disorder,

00:47.153 --> 00:53.191
and only STRING links from experiments
and databases, not text mining.

00:53.324 --> 00:56.500
The Leiden algorithm groups genes that interact

00:56.634 --> 00:59.304
more with each other than with the rest.

00:59.437 --> 01:02.864
No phenotype data are used.

01:03.367 --> 01:06.554
In the Clusters menu, choose one cluster.

01:06.687 --> 01:12.246
The network,
table and summary then show only that cluster.

01:12.767 --> 01:17.705
Term enrichment lists the terms
over-represented in the cluster.

01:17.838 --> 01:20.881
#Genes counts the members with a term.

01:21.014 --> 01:26.227
For the false discovery rate, FDR, smaller is stronger.

01:26.733 --> 01:29.186
The summary is marked AI.

01:29.320 --> 01:32.915
A language model wrote it,
and a second model checked it.

01:33.049 --> 01:39.417
AI evaluated means the check accepted the text,
not that a curator reviewed it.

01:39.550 --> 01:43.649
Its key themes open with chromatin remodeling.

01:44.167 --> 01:49.242
<i>ARID1B</i>, <i>CHD7</i> and <i>KMT2D</i>
share this chromatin cluster,

01:49.375 --> 01:53.466
yet they cause Coffin-Siris, CHARGE and Kabuki syndrome.

01:53.600 --> 01:59.454
A shared cluster suggests a shared process,
not the same disorder.

01:59.588 --> 02:02.327
Its name is a hypothesis.

02:02.833 --> 02:08.669
Back in the Analyses menu,
open Phenotype–function correlation.

02:08.802 --> 02:12.854
It compares functional clusters with phenotype clusters,

02:12.987 --> 02:16.913
which group entities with similar phenotypes.

02:17.433 --> 02:20.652
Every cluster appears on both axes.

02:20.786 --> 02:25.751
Skip the red diagonal:
it compares each cluster with itself.

02:25.885 --> 02:30.234
Read the bottom rows, where phenotypes meet function.

02:30.767 --> 02:32.238
Point to a cell there.

02:32.371 --> 02:37.822
The tooltip names both clusters,
pc for phenotype and fc for function,

02:37.955 --> 02:39.589
and their correlation.

02:39.723 --> 02:45.727
Red means they share more genes
than by chance; blue, fewer.

02:46.233 --> 02:48.572
How far can you trust a cell?

02:48.705 --> 02:51.559
It shows shared genes, not why.

02:51.692 --> 02:55.126
Some links are expected from how the clusters formed.

02:55.260 --> 02:58.985
Cluster numbers can change with each data version.

02:59.118 --> 03:02.633
Use a cell to ask questions.

03:03.133 --> 03:08.224
Remember: clusters come from protein interactions,
not phenotypes.

03:08.357 --> 03:13.578
A cluster name is a hypothesis;
the AI summary is not curation.

03:13.711 --> 03:17.454
A heatmap cell is a question to check.

03:17.967 --> 03:24.526
For more on both pages,
see the documentation chapter Read the analyses.
